Generic single-database configuration.

## How this is wired into the app

`app/migrations.py` calls Alembic automatically on every app startup (see
`main.lifespan()`) -- there is no manual step for the person launching the
desktop app. It resolves the DB URL the same way the app itself does
(app/config.py: DATABASE_URL env var override, or the persistent
LocalAppData path on Windows), so `alembic upgrade` always targets the same
database the app is about to serve.

`baseline_2_5_35` (alembic/versions/baseline_2_5_35_baseline_schema_2_5_35.py)
is the first migration and simply describes the schema as it already stood
in build 2.5.35 -- the schema that every existing database (fresh or
years-old) already has, thanks to the pre-Alembic `Base.metadata.create_all()`
+ hand-written `ALTER TABLE` guards still sitting in `main.lifespan()`. On
first contact with a database that has no `alembic_version` table yet,
`app/migrations.py` *stamps* it at `baseline_2_5_35` (writes one row, runs no
DDL) instead of trying to re-create tables that already exist. That old
ALTER TABLE bootstrap in main.py is intentionally left in place; it is not
being replaced retroactively.

## Adding a new schema change from here on

Don't add another `ALTER TABLE ...` block to `main.lifespan()`. Instead:

1. Change the model(s) in `app/models.py`.
2. From `backend/`, with the venv active:
   `alembic revision --autogenerate -m "short description"`
3. Open the generated file in `alembic/versions/` and check it -- autogenerate
   is a good first draft, not a guarantee (SQLite in particular sometimes
   needs the batch-mode `op.batch_alter_table(...)` form, which the env.py in
   this folder already enables by default via `render_as_batch=True`).
4. Test it locally: run the app once against a scratch copy of a real
   persistent `weee_dev.db` (never the user's live one) and confirm existing
   row counts are unchanged and the new column/table behaves as expected.
   The next app startup after that will apply it automatically for every
   real database via `alembic upgrade head` -- no manual step needed.
